\chapter{Tutorials}
\label{tut:tutorials}

This chapter collects the tutorials that ship inside the package as
the [[learnlog tutorial]] catalog.
The tangled Markdown under each section is the student-facing content
that [[pytorial]] runs; the woven prose around it documents how each
tutorial is designed to teach.

The catalog is ordered as a progression from student to researcher.
\Cref{tut:getting-started} sets up a project and closes the first
record--review loop; \cref{tut:playback-and-tagging} stays inside one
recorded project and adds the reading tools;
\cref{tut:export-and-share} moves a log between people and machines;
and \cref{tut:analysing-progsnap2} turns an exported dataset into a
report.
Across the catalog the recorded project is the invariant, and each
tutorial rotates one part of the CLI surface into
focus---creating, reading, moving, analysing---so a learner can
attribute what is new in each tutorial to that part alone.

The first three tutorials each come in two variants for two
audiences: a novice variant for learners who have never programmed
before, and an expert variant, suffixed [[-expert]] in the catalog,
for experienced programmers.
The variants share their object of learning---the workflow the
tutorial practises---and they share every step: the same titles, the
same commands, and the same machine-checked completion patterns.
What varies is the visibility of the mechanism.
For the expert, how the pieces work---fresh shells, per-shell
activation, the hidden Git repository---is part of the object of
learning; for the novice that layer is deliberately backgrounded,
because mechanism talk about tools the learner has no model of yet
adds load without adding discernment.
Each pair tangles from one literate source, and the shared parts are
shared noweb chunks rather than copies: the step checks are defined
once and referenced from both variants, so the two tutorials cannot
drift apart in what they ask the learner to do---only in how much
they explain.
The analysis tutorial has no novice variant: its learner is a teacher
or researcher analysing a dataset, already the expert end of the
progression.

